## ===========================================================================
## THE WARES — what the stalls sell, and the source's rule for stealing it
##
## Book 2's "Contents" chapters for the eight stalls that are not the Fruit
## Stall (`story.ni:2252-2950`), carried as the source has them: each stall's
## collective wares as an examinable, its displays as scenery a single
## corresponding item sits on, the item itself, and the refusals Gentry wrote
## for the things that cannot be nicked (the saddle, the swords, the hats, the
## gems). The Fruit Stall's own displays stay with the fruit in
## `grubbers-market.chord`, where the apple's and the banana's rulings live;
## this file is imported from there, after the fruit, so the wares stand in
## rooms already declared.
##
## THE THEFT RULE (`story.ni:1978-1998`, "Carry out stealing" and its two
## "After stealing" messages), as the change document makes it situational
## ("Theft — calm theft is quiet, chase theft is noisy"): while the market is
## calm a lift is quiet and nothing is blocked; once the mercenaries are
## sweeping (`hunted` or `chase`) the source's rule runs unchanged — the
## stallkeeper sees, Jack keeps the item (into the satchel), she is thrown
## into a random adjacent stall, and the stall is BLOCKED: coming back to it
## bounces her out again with Gentry's "As soon as you get near…" line
## (`story.ni:1825-1835`). The source picks one of two openers by whether
## Jack has stolen before ("a mere urchin" / "a successful thief") — that
## flag is the `thefts` counter below, raised by every noisy theft; the
## banana's fruit-stall theft raises it too (`grubbers-market.chord`), as
## the source's one `Carry out stealing` did for every steal. During Market
## Escape stealing is refused with the scene's own line (`story.ni:4160`),
## which gives `escape-no-time` its second consumer.
##
## HOW IT IS BUILT. One trait per stall (`grocer-ware`, `leather-ware`, …):
## a dispatch clause that names a room is a trait's, and the blocked stall
## is a static name. Every ware composes its stall's trait; the trait's
## `on the player taking` carries the openers and the `after` does the
## moving. The theft text names the item through `{the item}` — the taking
## action's own grammar slot, which the runtime binds into any `on the
## player taking` body (`runtime.ts:3044`); no `with` binding is needed
## (one could be written now — GH #335's `with … when` order parses since
## 2026-09-03). The reins are plural, so they carry their own pair of openers
## ("from their shelf") on a second leather trait.
##
## The displays are scenery supporters the item SITS on (the shape the
## fruit chose: the source's off-stage corresponding item materialised on
## theft has no port equivalent, so the item is simply there). A ware is
## `shelved` until it is taken and `nicked` after, so a dropped ware taken
## again is a plain take, as the source's on-stage `try taking` was.
##
## READINGS FLAGGED FOR DAVID — the change document's theft rule was written
## about the fruit; this file applies it to every stall as its wording says:
##   1. A calm lift of ANY ware is quiet — a knife from the weaponsmith, a
##      belt, a candle — with one TODO line for all of them
##      (`stall-lift-quietly`), since the source has no calm theft text at
##      all. The apple keeps David's own line.
##   2. A quiet calm lift does NOT make Jack a "successful thief" for the
##      source's opener choice — the same reading the banana took (the 2009
##      player was never `stealing` the apple she started with). The first
##      NOISY theft gets the urchin opener.
##   3. Eating a stolen grocery is refused with the source's "You don't have
##      time for breakfast now!" in every state — its scarf-down line
##      (`story.ni:2306`) sits behind an `Instead` that always fires, dead
##      code in 2009 too. In the remake's calm walk that reads oddly beside
##      the apple's appetite thread; carried as the source has it.
##
## The refusals on scenery TAKES — the saddle's "way too huge", the swords'
## "too big", the hats' "perfectly serviceable hat", the gems' "constantly
## glaring" — DO fire (probed 2026-08-30): a story `on the player taking`
## clause is consulted ahead of stdlib's scenery gate, so the older note
## with the fruit ("stdlib's `taking` refuses scenery inside `validate`,
## before any story hook") no longer describes the platform. The displays
## still carry their item as a supporter, which is the shape that lets the
## item be a real thing in the world.
##
## NOT CARRIED, with reasons:
##   * `steal <collective wares>` picking a random display (leather goods,
##     candles, pottery, the fruit bins) — no random-entity draw in Chord;
##     the collective wares refuse with `sticky-fingers` instead.
##   * The blocked tail on the stalls' distant descriptions ("The
##     stallkeeper looks like he's still keeping a sharp eye out for you") —
##     the peering phrases are `randomly` arms with no conditional tail.
##   * Bare `smell` in the Leather and Candle stalls (`story.ni:2557, 2795`)
##     — a bare verb reaches no entity clause (the same gap as the alley's
##     bare `listen`); the lines ride the stalls' wares and fumes instead.
##   * The knife-into-scabbard refusal (`story.ni:2599`) — a clause cannot
##     test which item is being inserted.
##   * Tasting (no stdlib action), peeling (`story.ni:2453`, a fruit verb
##     with no consumer here), the wax colour drawn at random for a bare
##     `steal wax candles` (`story.ni:2830`) — the four colours are four
##     displays and the parser asks which.
##   * `story.ni:1825`'s draw of an UNBLOCKED adjacent room — ADR-326's draw
##     skips blocked exits, not blocked rooms, so a throw can land Jack in a
##     stall that is itself blocked; she is not bounced until she walks out
##     and back in (an authorial move fires no entering clause).
##
## STRUCTURAL CHOICES David may rename at will: the two peg entities (`hat
## pegs`, `rope pegs` — the source's one backdrop in two rooms, split because
## an entity has one name); the display names where the source's printed
## name was the item's own word (`pyramid display`, `strips display`); and
## the bonnets carry no singular `bonnet` alias — that word is the
## fashionable hat's (`disguise.chord`), and the tree's pins on the trade
## name the hat by it (a `states:` pin takes one word).
## Every finished line is Gentry's; the marked TODO texts are David's.
## ===========================================================================

define counter thefts starts 0

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE SHARED REFUSALS AND THE THEFT TEXT
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

## `story.ni:1939-1945` — any handling of a display but examining or
## smelling, while the stallkeeper is there (he always is).

define phrase sticky-fingers
  'Hey!' the stallkeeper glares at you. 'Keep your sticky fingers away from
  the goods.'
end phrase

## DAVID: the quiet lift. The source has no calm theft text — its every
## theft was noisy — so one line stands in for every ware but the apple,
## which keeps your own.

define phrase stall-lift-quietly
  (TODO during play-testing — A quiet lift of {the item} from its display
  while the market is calm; the stallkeeper never looks up.)
end phrase

## `story.ni:1990` — the first noisy theft.

define phrase stall-theft-urchin
  Ah, *this* is something you know how to do, at least. You sidle up casually
  to the stall, wait until the stallkeeper seems preoccupied, then reach out
  and pluck {the item} from its shelf.

  Suddenly the stallkeeper jabs his finger at you and screams, *'Stop,
  thief!'*

  Apparently, he wasn't quite as preoccupied as you thought.

  You sprint through the crowd, stuffing {the item} into your satchel. This
  stallkeeper will be on the lookout for you now; probably best to stay away
  from this part of Grubber's for a while.
end phrase

## `story.ni:1997` — every noisy theft after the first.

define phrase stall-theft-thief
  With the stallkeepers all on edge about the mercenaries, it's not likely
  you'll be able to nick anything without someone noticing. Still, nothing
  ventured...

  *'Thief!'*

  Here you go again.

  You sprint through the crowd, stuffing {the item} into your satchel. This
  stallkeeper will be on the lookout for you now; probably best to stay away
  from this part of Grubber's for a while.
end phrase

## The same two, for the one plural ware (the reins): "from their shelf".

define phrase stall-theft-urchin-plural
  Ah, *this* is something you know how to do, at least. You sidle up casually
  to the stall, wait until the stallkeeper seems preoccupied, then reach out
  and pluck {the item} from their shelf.

  Suddenly the stallkeeper jabs his finger at you and screams, *'Stop,
  thief!'*

  Apparently, he wasn't quite as preoccupied as you thought.

  You sprint through the crowd, stuffing {the item} into your satchel. This
  stallkeeper will be on the lookout for you now; probably best to stay away
  from this part of Grubber's for a while.
end phrase

## `story.ni:1825-1835` — coming back to a stall whose keeper is watching
## for her.

define phrase stall-blocked-bounce
  As soon as you get near, the stallkeeper spots you and starts yelling
  again. Before the idiot can bring the mercenaries right to you, you dart
  away through the crowd.
end phrase

## `story.ni:1940` and `:2281` — the sticky-fingers rule's cousins for the
## displays: handling them is refused; only looking and smelling are free.

define trait stall-display
  on the player touching
    refuse sticky-fingers
  end on

  on the player pushing
    refuse sticky-fingers
  end on

  on the player pulling
    refuse sticky-fingers
  end on

  on the player searching
    refuse sticky-fingers
  end on

  on the player taking
    refuse sticky-fingers
  end on
end trait

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE GROCERY STALL (`story.ni:2252-2312`)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

define trait grocer-ware
  on the player taking while it is shelved
    refuse when the player's market-escape has started: escape-no-time
    phrase stall-lift-quietly when calm
    phrase stall-theft-urchin when (hunted or chase) and thefts is 0
    phrase stall-theft-thief when (hunted or chase) and thefts is at least 1
  end on

  after the player taking while it is shelved
    change it to nicked
    move it to the cloth satchel when hunted or chase
    raise thefts by 1 when hunted or chase
    change the Grocery Stall to blocked when hunted or chase
    move the player to a random adjacent room when hunted or chase
  end after
end trait

create the baskets
  aka basket, display, displays, fare, grocer's displays, wares, merchandise, goods
  scenery, plural
  in the Grocery Stall

  There are baskets bristling with loaves of bread, yellow pyramids of cheese,
  and spiced jerky hanging in bundles overhead.

  on the player taking
    refuse sticky-fingers
  end on

  on the player smelling
    phrase food-smells-tasty
  end on

create the display of loaves
  aka loaves, loaves of bread, dark loaves, grainy loaves, rock-hard loaves, bread display
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  stall-display
  in the Grocery Stall

  The loaves are dark and grainy and look rock-hard.

  on the player smelling
    phrase food-smells-tasty
  end on

  on the player eating
    refuse no-time-for-breakfast
  end on

create the pyramid display
  aka pyramids, pyramids of cheese, cheese pyramids, yellow pyramids, cheese display
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  stall-display
  in the Grocery Stall

  The cheese is a rich, milky-yellow color, with a thick, brown rind.

  on the player smelling
    phrase food-smells-tasty
  end on

  on the player eating
    refuse no-time-for-breakfast
  end on

create the strips display
  aka strips, strips of jerky, bundles, bundles of jerky, spiced jerky, jerky display
  scenery, a supporter, plural
  stall-display
  in the Grocery Stall

  The jerky hangs in dried, shriveled strips, and looks just slightly more
  appetizing than eating your own belt.

  on the player smelling
    phrase food-smells-tasty
  end on

  on the player eating
    refuse no-time-for-breakfast
  end on

create the loaf
  aka bread, loaf of bread, dark loaf, grainy loaf
  edible
  grocer-ware
  states: shelved, nicked
  on the display of loaves

  The loaf is dark and grainy and looks rock-hard.

  on the player smelling
    phrase food-smells-tasty
  end on

  on the player eating
    refuse no-time-for-breakfast
  end on

create the cheese
  aka wedge of cheese, yellow cheese, milky cheese
  edible
  grocer-ware
  states: shelved, nicked
  on the pyramid display

  The cheese is a rich, milky-yellow color, with a thick, brown rind.

  on the player smelling
    phrase food-smells-tasty
  end on

  on the player eating
    refuse no-time-for-breakfast
  end on

create the strip of beef jerky
  aka jerky, strip of jerky, beef jerky, beef, strip, salt-beef, dried beef
  edible
  grocer-ware
  states: shelved, nicked
  on the strips display

  It's a twisted, shriveled strip of dried salt-beef. Yum.

  on the player smelling
    phrase food-smells-tasty
  end on

  on the player eating
    refuse no-time-for-breakfast
  end on

define phrase food-smells-tasty
  The food smells tasty!
end phrase

define phrase no-time-for-breakfast
  You don't have time for breakfast now!
end phrase

## Kept aside, unreachable in the source as well (`story.ni:2306`, behind
## the always-firing refusal above): "You scarf [the noun] down. Dry, but
## tasty."

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## THE HAT STALL (`story.ni:2505-2544`) — nothing here can be nicked
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

create the hat pegs
  aka wooden pegs, pegs, peg, wood pegs, scores of pegs
  scenery, plural
  in the Hat Stall

  There's nothing special about the wooden pegs; they're just there to hold
  the hats up.

create the stock of hats
  aka hats, headgear, forest of headgear, varied headgear, display, displays, fare, wares, merchandise, goods
  scenery, plural
  in the Hat Stall

  If you can wear it on your head, it's sold here. Big, flop-brimmed traveling
  hats; small, leather workman's caps; wide *sappans* of woven straw from the
  Kozar delta; fancy, feathered hats for courtly balls; and even a few
  ordinary bonnets.

  on the player taking
    refuse hats-serviceable
  end on

create the traveling hats
  aka traveling hat, travelling hats, travelling hat, floppy hats, flop-brimmed hats, brimmed hats
  scenery, plural
  in the Hat Stall

  The traveling hats have big, floppy brims to keep the sun out of your eyes
  while on the road.

  on the player taking
    refuse hats-serviceable
  end on

create the workmans caps
  aka workman's caps, workmans cap, workman's cap, leather caps, leather cap, caps
  scenery, plural
  in the Hat Stall

  These leather caps are small and functional, more to protect your head from
  things falling on it than anything else.

  on the player taking
    refuse hats-serviceable
  end on

create the sappans
  aka sappan, straw hats, straw hat, woven hats, round hats, pointy hats, kozarian hats
  scenery, plural
  in the Hat Stall

  The *sappan* is a wide, round hat made of woven straw, pointy at the top.
  They're popular in the southern regions. You never see anyone wearing them
  in this part of the kingdom.

  on the player taking
    refuse hats-serviceable
  end on

create the fancy hats
  aka fancy hat, feathered hats, feathered hat, courtly hats, gaudy hats, cheap hats
  scenery, plural
  in the Hat Stall

  At a closer look, the 'fancy' hats are actually sort of cheap and gaudy.
  Anyone who is attending a real courtly ball probably wouldn't be shopping
  for hats in Grubber's Market anyway.

  on the player taking
    refuse hats-serviceable
  end on

create the bonnets
  aka white caps, peasant bonnets, ordinary bonnets
  scenery, plural
  in the Hat Stall

  The bonnets are just ordinary peasant wear, little white caps that tie
  under the chin.

  on the player taking
    refuse hats-serviceable
  end on

define phrase hats-serviceable
  You already have a perfectly serviceable hat. And anyway, you're going to
  need more than just a new hat to fool these mercenaries.
end phrase

